The Django application in appointments-app/ was provided by the Amazon Cloud
Institute (ACI) as starter code for Capstone 2. All source in that directory
is the work of ACI, not of this repository's author.

This remains true of the Lab 3 changes inside appointments-app/ — the DynamoDB
announcements read in appointments/views.py, the announcements block in
appointments/templates/appointments/index.html, and the mock_scan and
test_announcements additions in appointments/tests.py. Those follow ACI lab
instructions and are ACI's content. The author's own edits in that directory are
incidental adaptations for local tooling and CI: reading the table name from the
ANNOUNCEMENTS_TABLE environment variable, the PYTHONPATH prefix in
local_build.sh, and the corresponding fixes in buildspecs/buildspec_unittest.yml
(the PYTHONPATH prefix, the $CODEBUILD_SRC_DIR phase paths, the report
base-directory entries, and the Python 3.12 runtime).

The same is true of the Lab 5 changes inside appointments-app/ — the RDS DATABASES
block in hairdresser_django/settings.py and the Dockerfile. Both follow ACI lab
instructions and are ACI's content. The author's edits there are incidental: in
settings.py, guarding the RDS block on the DATABASE_* environment variables so the
test suite keeps using SQLite; in the Dockerfile, the python:3.12-slim base image in
place of 3.11-slim and the apt-get package-list cleanup. Some of the Dockerfile's
explanatory comments are the author's, added alongside ACI's.

It is true again of the Lab 6 change inside appointments-app/ —
buildspecs/buildspec_buildimage.yml. ACI supplied the file as a skeleton with the
commands left blank as "# Insert a command to..." exercises; the phase structure,
those guide comments, and the completed commands follow ACI's instructions and
solution, so the file is ACI's content. The author's edits are adaptations to a
repository shaped differently from the lab's: reading the ECR registry from the
$ECR_REPO_URL environment variable that modules/codebuild_buildimage injects, rather
than assembling it from a hardcoded repository name and an account ID looked up with
sts:GetCallerIdentity; trimming that value to a hostname for docker login; adding
`cd $CODEBUILD_SRC_DIR/appointments-app`, which the lab does not need because there
the repository root is the Django app itself; and correcting a duplicated "Build
started" log line, the post_build indentation, and a comment naming CodeCommit where
this repository uses GitHub. The file header records the same list.

It is true once more of the Lab 7 change inside appointments-app/ —
manifests/appointments-deployment.yml and manifests/appointments-service.yml. ACI
supplied both files with INSERT placeholders for the student to complete, so their
structure and field choices are ACI's content. The author's edits are adaptations to
this repository's own infrastructure: targetPort 8088 in the service rather than the
lab's 8080, matching the port this repository's Dockerfile exposes; and the
LIBMYSQL_ENABLE_CLEARTEXT_PLUGIN environment variable in the deployment, which the lab
does not need because its database uses a password while this one authenticates with an
IAM token. The remaining placeholders are left unfilled on purpose — the real values
carry the AWS account ID and the RDS endpoint, which this repository keeps out of git.

Both files are now commented out and applied by neither kubectl nor Terraform. They are
retained as the Lab 7 deliverable, each with a header recording what it describes and
which Terraform resource superseded it. The replacements —
infrastructure/modules/eks/k8s_service.tf and k8s_deployment.tf — are the author's own
work: they express the same two Kubernetes objects through the hashicorp/kubernetes
provider, with the placeholder values supplied from the ecr and rds modules' outputs
instead of by hand. The Service moved because type LoadBalancer provisions a billable AWS
load balancer that kubectl leaves orphaned when the cluster is destroyed; the Deployment
moved because its values are outputs of the same Terraform run. ACI supplied no Terraform
for either, so no ACI content carried across the boundary — only the field values the lab
had already specified, which appear identically in the commented manifests above.

A third file in that directory, manifests/appointments-serviceaccount.yml, is the author's
own work rather than ACI's. The lab creates the appointments-sa service account during its
own provisioning and never shows the student a manifest for it, so there is no ACI original
to adapt. It exists here because this repository provisions its own cluster and must create
that account itself, and because the EKS Pod Identity association in infrastructure/ matches
pods by that exact name.

Everything in infrastructure/ is the original work of this repository's author:
the Terraform module and environment layout; the DynamoDB table configuration; the
codebuild_unittest module with its IAM service role and policies, CloudWatch log group,
webhook, and test and coverage report groups; the codebuild_buildimage module with its
role, policies, log group, and ECR push permissions; the CodePipeline module with its
service role, artifact bucket, GitHub source trigger, and three stages; the ECR
repository module; the RDS module with its security group and the IAM database
authentication wiring in envs/dev; the EKS module with its cluster, managed node group,
cluster and node IAM roles, and the eks_enabled switch in envs/dev that destroys it on
demand; the CodeConnections wiring to GitHub; and the S3 remote state backend.

The EKS module deserves a specific note. The ACI lab did not ask for a cluster to be
built: it assumed one named eks-cluster already provisioned in the lab account and asked
the student only to verify it with kubectl and eksctl. No lab instructions, skeleton, or
solution exist for creating one. This repository has no such pre-provisioned cluster, so
the author researched and wrote the cluster, node group, IAM roles and trust policies,
subnet selection, endpoint access configuration, and the eks_enabled destroy switch from
scratch. The lab's verification commands are the only part that traces to ACI, and they
are commands to run, not code.

The same holds for the Lab 9 load balancer work. ACI's Lab 9 installs the AWS Load
Balancer Controller with the helm CLI and supplies an ingress manifest
(appointments-ingress.yml) for the student to apply with kubectl, then changes the service
manifest's type to NodePort. This repository applies neither: the controller is installed by
a helm_release resource and the Ingress is a kubernetes_ingress_v1 resource, both in
infrastructure/modules/eks/, together with the subnet tags, the controller's IAM role and
Pod Identity association, and the chart values that let it run without instance metadata —
all the author's own work. The Ingress carries the field values the lab's manifest specified
(its name, the internet-facing scheme and ip target-type annotations, the alb ingress class,
and the / prefix routed to appointments-service on port 80); as with Lab 7, only those values
crossed the boundary, and ACI supplied no Terraform. The Service is ClusterIP rather than the
lab's NodePort, because ip-mode targets route to pod IPs directly.

One file inside infrastructure/ is neither the author's nor ACI's.
infrastructure/modules/eks/alb_controller_iam_policy_v3.5.0.json is vendored verbatim from the
AWS Load Balancer Controller project at tag v3.5.0
(kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller, docs/install/iam_policy.json), which is
licensed Apache-2.0 — the same license as this repository. It is the IAM policy that
project publishes for its own controller, so it is reproduced unmodified rather than
rewritten; a hand-edited copy would silently drift from what the controller actually
calls. JSON has no comment syntax, so its provenance is recorded here and in the
Terraform resource that loads it. ACI's Lab 9 does not supply this file: in the lab the
policy was pre-attached to a service account in the provisioned account, and the student
never sees it.

Two files outside infrastructure/ are also the author's own work, not ACI's:
appointments-app/COMMANDS.md, an annotated reference for the local, Docker, ECR, and
kubectl workflows, written for this repository and this machine; and the root README.md.

Any further files added by the repository author will be noted here as they are
introduced.
